  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (Through Sunday)...\n\nLow Elevation Rain...\nConfidence in the rainfall forecast has increased. Models are in\ngood agreement that a lob of low pressure will form over \nSoutheast Montana, enhancing rainfall to the north and west of \nthis low. This puts locations in Custer/Fallon Counties and north\nto Glendive in the most favorable zone to see heavy rainfall of \n1.5-2 inches or greater. This corridor of heaviest rainfall has \nshifted to the north compared to previous forecasts. The lightest \nrainfall amounts will be north and west of Billings where \ngenerally 0.25-0.50 inches of rainfall are expected. Most \nlocations south and east of Billings have a moderate to high \nchance of getting greater than 1 inch of rainfall. Precipitation \nchances will decrease from west to east across the region today \nwith rain having moved into the Dakotas by tonight.\n\nMountain Snow...\nSnowfall will increase over the Absaroka/Beartooth and Bighorn\nMountains this morning. Snowfall totals exceeding 6 inches in the\nAbsaroka/Beartooths will be limited to north and west facing \nslopes over the highest elevations. Most of the Bighorn Mountains \ncan expect 5 to 10 inches of snow with winds gusting into the 40s \nmph. The combination of heavy snow and strong winds will lead to \nhazardous driving and recreation. Visibilities under 1/2 mile are \nexpected with occasional reductions under 1/4 mile. Travel along \nHighway 14 will be very difficult. Conditions are expected to \nimprove this evening with winds becoming light overnight. Snow \nlevels are expected to get down to around 5,000 ft bringing 0-2 \ninches of snow to places such as Story and Red Lodge. \n\nWind...\nA cold front moving in behind the low pressure system will bring\nwidespread 700 mb winds of 30-40 kts. Surface winds increase this\nmorning with most locations seeing a maximum wind gust in the 30s\nto low 40s mph range today. The strongest winds will be along the\nDakotas border and North Central Wyoming where wind gusts in \nexcess of 50 mph are expected (50-90% chance). There is a 30-60%\nchance that wind gusts will exceed 60 mph in southern Sheridan \nCounty. Winds will begin to decrease from west to east this \nevening with light winds expected across the region by Tuesday \nmorning. \n\nRest of the Week...\nTuesday through Sunday is expected to bring high temperatures in\nthe 50s with a limited chance of precipitation.",
